England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"12th century","h":16,"osm":"way/97416500","kind":"church","listEntry":1263869,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1263869","qid":"Q7590149","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/StMaryAttenborough.JPG","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Mary%27s_Church,_Attenborough","cat":"St Mary's Church, Attenborough","stories":[{"layer":"attenborough","text":"Henry Ireton, eldest son of German Ireton of Attenborough, was baptised in St Mary's Church here on 3 November 1611. He fought for Parliament at Edgehill in October 1642. On the night before Naseby, in June 1645, he surprised the Royalist army; the next day he was wounded and captured, and Cromwell later freed him. He married Bridget, Cromwell's eldest daughter, was one of the commissioners who signed the death warrant of Charles I, and died of disease outside Limerick on 26 November 1651.","source":{"title":"Henry Ireton","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Ireton","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["Charles I","note:Charles I"]],"links":["Charles I"],"when":"1611","whenSrc":{"title":"Henry Ireton","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Ireton","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Henry Ireton"},{"layer":"attenborough","text":"In St Mary's churchyard a memorial remembers the 134 people killed on 1 July 1918 (137 by another count)[^count] in the explosion at the shell factory in Chilwell, still the largest number of deaths caused by a single explosion in mainland Britain.","source":{"title":"Attenborough, Nottinghamshire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attenborough,_Nottinghamshire","kind":"Wikipedia"},"cites":{"count":{"title":"Shell Crisis of 1915","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shell_Crisis_of_1915","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1918","whenSrc":{"title":"Attenborough, Nottinghamshire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attenborough,_Nottinghamshire","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"The Chilwell explosion memorial","see":[